### 3.1 The previously missing upper-envelope step

Let P have a constant-Jacobian polynomial mate and unique top-total monomial lambda*x^a*y^b with a,b>0. Then deg_x P<=a and deg_y P<=b.

Suppose a support point(i,j) has i>a. Necessarily j<b and (i-a)/(b-j)<1. Let t be the maximum of these finitely many ratios and choose positive primitive integers rho>sigma with sigma/rho=t. The(rho,sigma)-face contains(a,b) and at least one point to its right and below. Every point with i<=a,j>b has strictly smaller weight, since the total-degree leader is unique; all other nonattaining points do too. Thus this face is nonmonomial and its counterclockwise END is(a,b).

The polynomial Euler element E has weight rho+sigma. Since rho>sigma>0, its only possible monomials are xy and, if integral, y^(1+rho/sigma). If the latter is absent, E is a monomial, which is incompatible with this nonmonomial face by the Euler-element monomial property. If present, its END is(0,1+rho/sigma), neither(1,1) nor parallel to(a,b). This contradicts the Euler endpoint condition. No i>a is possible. Interchanging x,y gives the other bound. All weights used here are strictly positive, and E is POLYNOMIAL; no Laurent clause is imported.

### 3.2 Applying it to the actual all-D hypotheses

The charged admissibility report imports McKay–Wang's constant-Jacobian two-factor bound and proves the centralizer's scalar-extension property. Over an algebraic closure these imply that H has exactly two distinct linear factors with coprime multiplicities r,s. One factor would make its linear root a forbidden centralizer element; a common exponent divisor would likewise give a forbidden proper root. Since r+s=D>=3, coprimality forces r!=s. Order the two linear coordinates so0<r<s. A nonzero scalar in H can be carried by target scalings; the Jacobian remains a nonzero constant.

In these coordinates the tops are x^(3r)y^(3s) and x^(5r)y^(5s). Section3.1 puts both supports in their corresponding rectangles. Therefore their x-degree ratio as well as total-degree ratio is3:5, and en_(1,0)(A)=(3r,3s), strictly above the diagonal. They are an actual(3,5)-pair in the polynomial ring, not merely a numerical degree match. Polynomial standardization preserves this endpoint. Its normalized endpoint is consequently(r,s), contradicting gcd(r,s)=1 versus the standard-pair restriction gcd>2.

This argument never uses the gcd-MINIMAL normalization of Proposition4.7. It passes through Definition4.3 directly and then Proposition5.20. It therefore addresses the precise caution in the frozen admissibility report rather than ignoring it. For arbitrary coefficient fields, the field-point use of McKay–Wang is transported through a finitely generated characteristic-zero coefficient field embedded in C, exactly as the charged admissibility report explains. No assertion over nilpotent rings follows from this proof.

